is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r3
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: The physical/link layer on collision domain J between r3 and r9; specifically r3’s interface eth0. Mechanism: r9 originates OSPF routes for 19.0.0.0/8, 39.0.0.0/8, and 90.0.0.0/8 (confirmed in r9’s FRR config: “network 19.0.0.0/8, 39.0.0.0/8, 90.0.0.0/8 area 0”). When the OSPF adjacency between r3 and r9 drops, those LSAs are withdrawn from the area, so r1 loses the routes or sees them flap. Localization: On r3, eth0 (the interface on link J to r9) is down: “ip addr” shows eth0 state DOWN and OSPF does not list eth0 among active interfaces (r3 “show ip ospf interface” only shows eth1/eth2/eth3). r3’s neighbor table has no adjacency to 39.1.1.2, while r9’s “show ip ospf neighbor” is empty. Meanwhile r9’s eth0 is up with 39.1.1.2/8, but with no neighbor. Correlation to r1: r1 has no routes for 19/8, 39/8, 90/8 (“% Network not in table”), matching loss of r9’s LSAs via r3. Root cause: The J link is broken because r3’s eth0 is down after maintenance (administratively down or disconnected), severing the only OSPF adjacency for r9. Corrective fix: Restore the J link by bringing r3 eth0 up (e.g., ip link set dev eth0 up) or fixing the cabling/tap, ensure OSPF on 39.0.0.0/8 remains enabled, and verify the r3–r9 adjacency forms; r1 should then consistently receive 19/8, 39/8, and 90/8 again. === END ===